    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "A key process shaping neurodevelopment is competition between individual axons for limited neurotrophic support. Local deprivation of trophic factors triggers caspase-dependent axon degeneration, which is carried out through apoptotic machinery present in axons. Although some effectors of degeneration have been defined, the complete pathways and molecular mechanisms remain undiscovered. In this project, I looked at two aspects of axon degeneration. First, in looking for potential effectors, I performed a CRISPR knockout screen to identify key effectors necessary for degeneration. I successfully identified Rufy3 as a neuron-specific effector in this caspase-based degeneration pathway, confirming an observation made in a preliminary shRNA screen. Next, I examined the upstream activating role of p53. Seeking to understand the temporal and spatial details of its involvement, I tested the effects of various p53 overexpression constructs on caspase activity. I demonstrated that cytoplasmic function of p53 is important for caspase activity, possibly through an acute mechanism. Taken together, these results provide new insights into the molecular mechanisms of programmed degeneration, a process which may contribute to the underlying pathology of neurodevelopmental disorders and neurodegenerative disease.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
